# Chroma library, modern Earth: LIFE EVENTS a world event brings (Library thread, 2026-10-09, "v22" update stage 3,
# items C4 "Nature's own year" and T4 "Good and bad, like life"). Design: chroma-world/model/stage3-rules.md section 5
# C4 (bad air, a poisoned river, drought, a glorious spring, recovery after a disaster) and section 4 T4 (a boom year, a
# good year in town, a good harvest, a reform that opens a door, a festival year; the glorious spring counts for both).
# Fields: chroma-engine/world-fields.md.
#
# The Engine fires each of these on its world event, only for the people the event's selector names (place unless the
# '# world:' line says otherwise); they have no background rate (per_year all 0, no drivers). The '# world:' line above
# each moment gives the event key, the selector and the share of selected people who meet the moment (estimate).
# Tone: three bad (bad air, the river, the dry year), one mixed (the rebuilding), six good or joy-led (spring and the
# five T4 moments), so with the world's existing shocks good and bad world events come out about even.
# Layout: each a life event, even alpha, the five one-colour options plus one half of a pair line; the ten use both
# halves of D1, D2, D3, D4 and D6, so every pair appears equally. Flags: habit once per colour, nothing else.
# Syntax: header of build.py.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C4: bad air (pairs D1a)

# world: bad air, selector place, share .3
== a season of bad air
tier: life event
where: city, industry
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 2-6
times: World event: bad air, selector place. The Engine's start rate is 30 in 100 a year in cities and industrial places, times one plus the heat extremes (stage3-rules C4); about 3 in 10 of the people living there feel it enough for it to be a moment (share .3, estimate), so in such a place a person meets it about once in 10 years (estimate)
likelier: a home on a busy road or near a works, a hot still summer, weak lungs, small children or old parents at home
rarer: a home far from the main roads, a cool windy year, work that keeps one indoors
tone: trouble
life: health, home, community
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, child, friend, neighbours
earth: you live through a summer when the air over the town turns thick and brown, and the doctors tell people to stay indoors
scene: For weeks a brown haze has hung over {place}. The air tastes of metal, the clinic is full of coughing people, and the morning news gives the air a number, like a fever.
scene W: The council has put out its advice: windows shut, no running outside, the old and the very young kept in. {N} knows advice only works if everyone keeps it.
scene U: {N} has found the sensor readings online, street by street, and the worst of them sit right beside the plant on the ring road.
scene B: The shop on the corner sold out of masks by noon and is charging double for the last box. {N} notices who is doing well out of the haze.
scene R: {N} is sick of being told to stay in. Everyone is short-tempered, and {friend} wants to go out anyway.
scene G: {grandparent} wheezes on the stairs, and {N} thinks of the hills outside town, where the air is still clean.
- W | keep the household to the council's advice, windows shut and outings short | v: conformity, security | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | chance: 80
- U | check the readings each morning and plan every outing around the cleanest hours | habit | v: self-direction, security | chance: 85
- B | buy a good air filter early, before the price climbs, and sell on the spare | v: achievement, power | chance: 85
- R | go out anyway and run your usual route, haze or no haze | habit | body: light | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 70
- G | take the family to the relatives in the hills until the haze lifts | v: security, benevolence | lever: exit | pushes: place | chance: 60
- WU | gather the street's readings and take them to the council meeting | v: universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 55
- WR | join the march to the plant gates demanding they cut the smoke | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | chance: 45
- UG | plant a thick hedge by the road and fill the house with air-cleaning plants | v: universalism, security | chance: 65
- BR | take the cleaning-crew work nobody else wants this summer, at triple pay | body: light | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 75
- BG | wait it out indoors and use the weeks to put your own affairs in order | v: security, achievement | lever: neglect | pushes: place | chance: 80
works: The haze lifts with the first autumn rain, and the household comes through it with nothing worse than a cough.
works: The fuss over the air does some good: the council moves the lorries off the school road.
fails: {N} spends three weeks with a chest that will not clear, in and out of the doctor's waiting room.
fails: Nothing changes at the plant, and next summer the haze comes back thicker.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C4: a poisoned river (pairs D2a)

# world: poisoned river (and bad water), selector place, share .4
== the river runs foul
tier: life event
where: industry, mining
at: council
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-20
times: World event: poisoned river (also bad water), selector place. The Engine's start rate in industrial and mining places is 2 in 100 a year for bad water and 5 in 1,000 for a poisoned river (stage3-rules C4); the fight over the blame reaches about 4 in 10 residents (share .4, estimate), so about once in a long life in such a place (estimate)
likelier: a home downstream of a works or a mine, weak oversight, a firm that employs much of the town, a family that fishes or farms by the water
rarer: a town with strong inspectors, a home far from the river, a firm with nothing to hide
tone: trouble
life: community, health, home
horizon: months
roles: boss, friend, colleague, neighbours, parent
earth: the river through your town is fouled by the works upstream, and the town fights over who is to blame
scene: The fish came up belly-first on Tuesday. By Thursday the tap water smells of chemicals, the council has put warning signs along the bank, and everyone in {place} has a theory about whose pipe it came from.
scene W: The works has a permit, and the permit has rules. Someone broke them, and {N} wants it named and put right properly.
scene U: {N} has seen the stain in the water below the outfall, and wants a proper sample tested before anyone starts shouting.
scene B: The works employs half the town, {boss} included. Whoever wins the fight over the blame will have a say over {place} for years.
scene R: {N} grew up swimming in that river. Somebody poisoned it, and {N} is furious.
scene G: The herons have gone from the bend below the bridge. {N} wonders how many years the river will need to heal.
- W | file a formal complaint and ask the council to enforce the works' permit | v: conformity, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 70
- U | take water samples at the outfall and send them away for testing | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | speak for the works at the council hearing, and keep your place on its good side | v: power, security | mark: made an enemy | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution employer | chance: 75
- R | stand up at the council meeting and name the firm to its face | v: stimulation, universalism | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 60
- G | buy water in bottles, keep everyone away from the river, and wait for it to clear | v: security, benevolence | lever: neglect | pushes: place | chance: 80
- WU | help draft the council's report on what went wrong and who must pay | v: conformity, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 50
- WG | get the neighbours together to clear the dead fish and fence off the worst stretch | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 70
- UR | post the test results online, with the firm's name, before anyone can bury them | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | chance: 55
- BR | sign up to the group claim against the firm and push hard for a payout | v: power, achievement | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | chance: 60
- BG | sell your plot by the river quietly, before the prices fall | v: security, power | lever: exit | pushes: place | chance: 55
works: The firm pays for the clean-up, and by the next summer children are swimming at the bend again.
works: The fight over the blame ends in new rules at the works, and {N} had a hand in them.
fails: The firm blames a contractor, the contractor blames the rain, and nobody pays.
fails: The quarrel splits the street between those who work at the plant and those who do not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C4: drought (pairs D3a)

# world: drought, selector place, share .5
== a dry year on the land
tier: life event
where: farming
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 4-12
times: World event: drought, selector place. The Engine's start rate in farming and hotter places is 8 in 100 a year, times the warming factor (stage3-rules C4); about half of the people living there feel it in prices or work (share .5, estimate), so in a farming place about once in 25 years (estimate)
likelier: a farming family, work on the land, a hot region, a warming climate, a household with little saved
rarer: a town job, irrigated land, savings to tide over a lean year
tone: trouble
life: work, money, home
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, friend, neighbours, boss
earth: the rain does not come for a year, the fields crack, food costs more, and the farms around your town cut back
scene: No rain since early spring. The fields around {place} are cracked and pale, the reservoir shows its old walls, and bread costs a little more every week. On the farms, the talk is of who will be laid off first.
scene W: There is a hosepipe ban and a rota for the reservoir. {N} knows it only works if nobody cheats.
scene U: {N} reads up on farming in dry country: mulch, drip lines, crops that need less. There must be a better way than waiting for rain.
scene B: Hay is selling at three times its price, and {N} knows a farmer up the valley who has to sell.
scene R: {N} cannot stand another month of watching an empty sky, and the farm work is drying up as fast as the fields.
scene G: {grandparent} remembers the last dry year, and says the land always comes back if it is left to rest.
- W | keep to the water rota, and report the neighbour who fills a pool at night | v: conformity, security | mark: made an enemy | chance: 80
- U | rig drip lines and mulch so the garden needs half the water | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | buy hay cheap from a farmer who has to sell, and sell it on in winter | v: power, achievement | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 75
- R | pack a bag and go to the city for work until the rain comes back | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: left home | lever: exit | pushes: place | chance: 65
- G | help the family grow only what the dry land will carry this year | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 70
- WU | help the council share out the reservoir fairly between farms and homes | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 50
- WR | take a night shift on the fire watch in the dry hills | body: light | v: benevolence, security | chance: 70
- UB | work out which crops will still pay, and plant them before anyone else does | v: achievement | chance: 55
- RG | haul water up to the farm by hand every morning to keep the animals alive | body: heavy | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 60
- BG | tighten the household belt, spend nothing, and sit the year out | v: security | chance: 80
works: The rain comes back in the autumn, and {N} has kept enough going to start again.
works: The dry year teaches {N} things about water and land that will matter for years.
fails: Prices stay high all winter, and the household runs down what it had saved.
fails: The farm that employs half the family lays people off before the harvest.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C4 and T4: a glorious spring (pairs D1b)

# world: glorious spring, selector place, share .15
== a glorious spring
tier: life event
time_of_year: spring
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 4-10
times: World event: glorious spring, selector place. The Engine's start rate is 15 in 100 a year everywhere (stage3-rules C4, also a T4 good event); about 15 in 100 residents take it as a moment rather than just good weather (share .15, estimate), so about once in 40 years (estimate)
likelier: a long hard winter before it, a garden or a park nearby, a light heart, children or friends to share it with
rarer: a long working day indoors, illness, grief, a city with few parks
tone: joy
life: home, play, community
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, partner, friend, neighbours
earth: after a long grey winter, a spring so lovely comes that the whole town seems to walk more slowly and smile more
scene: After a long grey winter, spring arrives all at once. Blossom is out on every street in {place}, the evenings are warm and long, and strangers stop each other to talk about the light.
scene W: The neighbours are talking about a clean-up of the street and a shared meal at the end of it. {N} likes it when everyone pitches in.
scene U: {N} has noticed which birds came back first and which trees flowered early this year, and wants to know why.
scene B: The cafes are full, the market is busy, and everyone is in the mood to spend. {N} sees a chance.
scene R: The sun is out and {N} cannot sit still. {friend} has already sent a message about the lake.
scene G: {N} wakes to birdsong and lets the morning come slowly, as the season seems to ask.
- W | organise the street clean-up and the shared meal at the end of it | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- U | start a notebook of what flowers when, on long walks before work | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B | set up a stall at the weekend market while people are in the mood to spend | habit | v: achievement, power | chance: 80
- R | skip work on the first warm day and take everyone to the lake | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 85
- G | dig and plant the garden, and spend the long evenings sitting in it | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 80
- RG | sleep out under the stars with friends on the warmest night | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 80
- UB | use the light evenings to finish the course you put off all winter | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 65
- UR | take the bike out alone and ride somewhere you have never been | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- WB | get the street to back a community garden, and offer to run it | v: power, benevolence | chance: 55
- WG | plant trees along the school road with the children and the old folk of the street | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 75
works: For a few weeks life feels lighter, and {N} remembers that spring for years.
works: Something started in the good weather keeps going long after it.
fails: A late frost and a week of rain end it early, and the plans go back in the drawer.
fails: {N} crams too much into the good weeks, and they go by in a rush.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C4: recovery after a disaster (pairs D2b)

# world: recovery, selector place, share .4
== the town builds itself back
tier: life event
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 10-30
times: World event: recovery, selector place, when the place's disaster level falls back (stage3-rules C4); services come back over 1 to 3 years, and about 4 in 10 residents take part in or feel the rebuilding as a moment (share .4, estimate); the person's own losses are the disaster's moments, not this one
likelier: a home or work in the worst-hit part of town, a family that stayed, a trade the rebuilding needs, a strong council
rarer: a family that moved away after the disaster, a part of town the disaster spared
tone: mixed
life: community, home, work
horizon: years
roles: grandparent, parent, friend, colleague, neighbours
earth: a year after the disaster, your town rebuilds: the school reopens, the bus comes back, one service at a time
scene: A year on from the disaster, {place} is coming back one piece at a time. The school runs in portable rooms, the bus comes every other hour, and the clinic still shares a building with the library. Everyone is tired, and everyone is still here.
scene W: The council's rebuilding plan is pinned to the town hall door, with a date for every item. {N} wants it kept to, item by item.
scene U: {N} has read the plan closely and can see where the money will run short and what will be left out.
scene B: Rebuilding money is flowing in and contracts are being handed out. {N} can see who is getting them, and wants to be one of them.
scene R: {N} is tired of waiting on permits and committees. The playground is still a fenced heap of rubble.
scene G: The trees that came down are sprouting from their stumps. {N} thinks the town will heal the same way, slowly, from what is left.
- W | sit on the residents' committee that checks the rebuilding against the plan | v: conformity, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 75
- U | learn a building trade on the council's rebuilding course | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | bid for a small rebuilding contract while the money is flowing | v: power, achievement | chance: 70
- R | round up friends and clear the playground rubble yourselves one weekend | body: heavy | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 75
- G | keep to your old shops and your old bench, so the town has its life back | v: tradition, security | mark: stayed home | lever: loyalty | pushes: place | chance: 80
- RG | throw a street party among the ruins to mark a year since the disaster | v: hedonism, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- UB | find the gaps in the rebuilding grants and get your own repairs paid first | v: achievement, security | chance: 70
- UG | help plant the banks and hillsides so the land holds better next time | body: light | v: universalism | chance: 55
- WB | stand for the rebuilding board and make sure your side of town comes first | v: power, security | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 55
- WR | press the council at every meeting until the clinic has its own building again | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | chance: 50
works: Two years on, the school is back in its own building and the bus runs on time again.
works: {N} finds a part in the rebuilding, and {place} feels more like home than it did before.
fails: The money runs short, and the clinic stays in the library for another winter.
fails: The rebuilding drags on, and some of the families who left never come back.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- T4: a boom year (pairs D3b)

# world: boom year, selector all, share .2
== a boom year
tier: life event
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 6-15
times: World event: boom year, selector all, when the output gap stays a standard deviation above its long mean for four quarters (stage3-rules T4); about 2 in 10 people feel it as offers and rising pay (share .2, estimate), mostly people of working age, so about once or twice in a working life (estimate)
likelier: working age, a skill in demand, a growing town, a person open to moving
rarer: a person long retired, a declining town, a sector the boom passes by
tone: joy
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: parent, partner, friend, boss
earth: you live through a boom year: jobs are easy to find, pay is rising, and offers come to people who never had them before
scene: Everyone seems to be hiring. Pay is rising, the shops are busy, new buildings are going up, and {N} has had two job offers this month without asking for either.
scene W: {boss} is offering a raise to everyone who stays. {N} has always believed in loyalty, and for once it seems to pay.
scene U: {N} reads the business news and wonders how long it can last. Booms end; the only question is when.
scene B: Money is moving fast, and the people who move with it are getting rich. {N} wants a share before it stops.
scene R: A firm at the other end of the country wants {N} next month, with the move paid. Why not just go?
scene G: {N} has seen good years before, and the lean ones after them. {parent} says to fill the cupboard while the sun shines.
- W | stay with your employer and take the raise offered for staying | v: security, conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution employer | chance: 80
- U | take the offer that teaches you the most, even at lower pay | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | play two offers against each other until one pays far more | v: power, achievement | chance: 75
- R | take the job at the other end of the country and move within the month | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: moved away | lever: exit | pushes: place | chance: 70
- G | put the extra pay aside for the lean years that always follow | v: security, tradition | chance: 75
- BR | quit and start your own firm while customers are easy to find | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 55
- UG | use the good year to retrain for work that will last when the boom ends | v: security, self-direction | chance: 60
- UR | spend the extra on the long trip you always put off | v: hedonism, stimulation | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 80
- WB | push for promotion now, while the firm needs every good person | v: achievement, power | chance: 60
- WG | help a friend out of work get taken on at your place | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 65
works: The good year leaves {N} better off, with savings or skills that last past the boom.
works: The change turns out well, and {N} wonders why it took a boom to try it.
fails: The boom turns sooner than anyone said, and what looked like a sure thing goes sour.
fails: {N} stretches too far in the good months and spends the next year paying it back.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- T4: a good year in town (pairs D4a)

# world: good year in town, selector place, share .3
== the jobs come back to town
tier: life event
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 8-20
times: World event: good year in town, selector place, when local unemployment falls by a point within a year (stage3-rules T4); about 3 in 10 residents feel it as work coming back or people coming home (share .3, estimate), so a few times in a life in a town that rises and falls (estimate)
likelier: a town that went through hard years, working age, a family with people who left for work, a trade the new work needs
rarer: a town that never lost its work, a person long retired, a job outside the town
tone: joy
life: work, community, home
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, friend, colleague, neighbours
earth: work comes back to your town: the old works reopens, shops open on the high street, and people who left talk of coming home
scene: The old works on the edge of {place} has reopened under new owners, and they are hiring. A bakery has opened in the empty shop on the high street, and {sibling}, who left for work years ago, is talking about coming home.
scene W: The new owners want a steady crew that turns up. {N} knows how to be that.
scene U: {N} has read the new owners' plans and can see which skills they will need in two years, not just now.
scene B: Shops on the high street have been cheap for years. They will not be cheap for long.
scene R: For once something is happening in {place}, and {N} wants to be in the middle of it.
scene G: The town is coming back to life the way a field comes back after a hard winter. {N} wants the old families to share in it.
- W | sign on at the reopened works and be there on time every shift | habit | v: security, conformity | chance: 80
- U | train for the skilled work the new owners will need in two years | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | take on a cheap empty shop on the high street before the rents go up | v: power, achievement | mark: took a wild risk | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 75
- R | open a pop-up bar in the old station and see if it takes off | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 65
- G | ask the new owners to hire from the old families who stayed through the lean years | v: benevolence, tradition | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | chance: 55
- BG | put in a word for your cousins, so the family gets the good shifts | v: power, benevolence | chance: 70
- RG | call the ones who left and tell them to come home, there is work again | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 70
- UR | pitch a new idea to the owners at their open day | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 45
- WB | get a seat on the works' liaison board as the town's voice | v: power, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | chance: 55
- WU | help the council set up a jobs board so everyone hears of the openings | v: universalism | chance: 70
works: Within the year {place} feels alive again, and {N} has a footing in the new work.
works: People who left start coming back, and the street {N} grew up on fills up again.
fails: The new owners hire mostly from outside, and the old hands are left watching.
fails: The good start stalls after a season, and the high street empties out again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- T4: a good harvest (pairs D4b)

# world: good harvest, selector place, share .4
== a bumper harvest
tier: life event
where: farming
time_of_year: autumn
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-15
times: World event: good harvest, selector place, when the food price falls by the set amount (stage3-rules T4); written for farming places, where about 4 in 10 residents feel it as a moment (share .4, estimate); elsewhere it is a world line of cheap food, so a few times in a life on the land (estimate)
likelier: a farming family, work on the land, a kind year after lean ones, a garden of one's own
rarer: a town job, a household that buys everything in shops, a farm that grows what the glut has made cheap
tone: joy
life: work, home, community
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, friend, neighbours
earth: the harvest is the biggest anyone can remember: the barns are full, food is cheap, and the town has plenty
scene: The weather was kind all year, and the harvest is the biggest anyone in {place} can remember. The barns are full, the market stalls are piled high, and fruit is going for almost nothing.
scene W: The harvest supper is on Saturday and every household brings something. {N} wants it done properly.
scene U: {N} notices that grain prices have fallen so far the farms may barely break even. Plenty has its own problems.
scene B: Grain is cheap now, and it will not be in spring. {N} starts thinking about storage.
scene R: The fields are gold, the cider is new, and the whole valley wants to celebrate.
scene G: {grandparent} is already setting out jars on the kitchen table. Good years are for putting by.
- W | help run the harvest supper and see that every household gets a share | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- U | work out what a glut does to prices and plan next year's planting around it | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 70
- B | buy grain cheap now and store it to sell in the spring | v: power, achievement | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 80
- R | dance at the harvest fair until the band packs up | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 90
- G | spend the week bottling, drying and putting by for the winter | habit | v: tradition, security | chance: 75
- BR | drive a van of cheap produce to the city market and sell it fast | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 70
- UB | sign a contract now that fixes next year's price in your favour | v: security, power | chance: 70
- UG | save seed from the best plants, with notes on which did well | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 70
- WG | give the surplus to the food bank before it spoils | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 80
- WR | lead the harvest parade through the town, banners and all | v: tradition, stimulation | chance: 80
works: The household eats well all winter, and the cellar is still not empty by spring.
works: {N} remembers that autumn as a time when there was enough for everyone.
fails: The glut drives prices so low that the farms barely cover their costs.
fails: Half of what was put by spoils in a damp cellar before the winter is out.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- T4: a reform that opens a door (pairs D6a)

# world: law changed (toward permitting, any key), selector all, share .1
== a new law opens a door
tier: life event
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-15
times: World event: law changed toward permitting, or a right granted (the Engine's 'law changed' event with any law key), selector all (stage3-rules T4); about 1 in 10 people take it as a moment of their own (share .1, estimate), more where the law touches their own life; a few times in a life in reforming times (estimate). Written for any key, so no option carries law:
likelier: a law that touches one's own life or a close person's, a reform wave, a family split on the question
rarer: a law far from one's own life, a closed regime, a settled law book
tone: mixed
life: public life, home, community
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, partner, friend, neighbours
earth: a law changes: something forbidden all your life is now allowed, and the people around you must decide what to make of it
scene: The new law came in at midnight. Something forbidden for as long as {N} can remember is now allowed, and the morning news shows people doing it openly, smiling, some of them in tears.
scene W: The law has changed, and the law is the law. {parent}, who argued against it for years, says it still does not make it right.
scene U: {N} reads the new law itself, every page, to see what it truly allows and what it does not.
scene B: A new freedom means a new market. Somebody will do well out of it, and {N} wonders who.
scene R: {N} has waited years for this. At last the choice is {Ns} own.
scene G: {grandparent} has seen the laws swing back and forth before. Customs, {grandparent} says, change more slowly.
- W | accept the new law, though you argued against it, and say so plainly | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | pushes: state | chance: 80
- U | read the new law in full and explain it to friends who are confused | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 85
- B | set up a small business serving what is newly allowed | v: achievement, power | mark: took a wild risk | requires: savings | without: means | chance: 70
- R | take the new freedom in the very first week, whatever anyone says | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 85
- G | keep to the old ways in your own house, and leave others to theirs | v: tradition | lever: neglect | pushes: state | chance: 70
- BG | wait to see who gains from it before taking any side | v: security, power | chance: 70
- UG | hear out the elders and the young on it before making up your mind | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 75
- UR | write openly online about what the change means for people like yourself | act: write openly online about what the change means for people like them | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: culture | chance: 60
- WB | join the campaign to hedge the new law about with rules and licences | v: security, power | lever: voice | pushes: state | chance: 45
- WR | march in the celebration that weekend with everyone it frees | v: universalism, stimulation | lever: voice | pushes: culture | chance: 80
works: The change settles in faster than anyone feared, and {N} finds a way to live with it.
works: A year on, the quarrels over it have quietened, and {N} is glad of where they stand.
fails: The family argues over it at every meal for months.
fails: The new freedom comes wrapped in more rules than anyone expected, and the first try goes badly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- T4: a festival year (pairs D6b)

# world: festival year, selector members (of the main faith), share .3
== a feast in a year of trust
tier: life event
holy: feast
at: faith body
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
gap: 5-15
times: World event: festival year, selector members of the main faith, when its holy weeks fall in a year of high trust (stage3-rules T4); about 3 in 10 members feel it as more than the usual feast (share .3, estimate), a handful of times in a life of faith (estimate)
likelier: a family that keeps the faith, a close congregation, good times, old quarrels ready to mend
rarer: a member in name only, a scattered family, a year of grief or illness
tone: joy
life: faith, home, community
horizon: week
roles: grandparent, parent, sibling, friend, neighbours
earth: the great feast comes round in a good year, the hall is full, and people who had drifted apart sit at the same tables
scene: The feast has come round again, and this year it feels different. Times are good and people trust each other. The hall is full, the tables run out into the street, and families who have not spoken in years are passing each other bread.
scene W: The old rites are kept properly this year, every prayer and every dish in its place. {N} feels part of something that will outlast everyone at the table.
scene U: {N} has been reading about where the feast's customs came from, and they are older than anyone at the table thinks.
scene B: Everyone who matters in {place} is at the feast. {N} knows that where a person sits tonight says a great deal.
scene R: Music, food, and the whole street out late. {N} means to enjoy every hour of it.
scene G: {grandparent} sits at the head of the table, as always, with the young ones all around. {N} looks around and feels at home.
- W | keep the whole fast and every rite before the feast, as the faith teaches | v: tradition, conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: belief | chance: 80
- U | find out what each custom of the feast means, and tell the young ones | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 80
- B | sit with the people who matter, and make the most of the evening | v: power, achievement | mark: made a friend | chance: 80
- R | dance in the street until dawn with everyone else | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 90
- G | cook the dishes your grandmother cooked and set a place for whoever comes | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 75
- BR | play host to the biggest table in the street, and enjoy every bit of the attention | v: hedonism, power | chance: 75
- RG | make it up with the cousin you fell out with years ago, there at the table | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 55
- UB | help keep the feast's accounts, and earn a say in how the money is spent | v: power, achievement | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution faith body | chance: 80
- WG | bring the lonely old neighbours to the feast and make room at your table | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 80
- WU | help the elders write down the feast's old customs before they are forgotten | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 80
works: The feast leaves {N} warm for weeks, closer to family and faith than before.
works: Something mended at the table stays mended, and the year ahead feels steadier.
fails: An old quarrel flares up across the table and spoils the evening.
fails: The crowds, the cooking and the late nights leave {N} worn out by the end of it.
